{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543744,"nonce":"K6waFb7VZudZu6rzPyVQm2Fq2y4=","target":"a71f32f16b3d1f0ced45415f22f4f0a6598b0f3fe53d62d897823da2e85aeb3c","message":"A problem that is second order in time (`u.tt`) cannot take part in a transient coupling. `Participant.initial()` raises `NotImplementedError(\"coupling: a u.tt problem marches [u; v]; its windows are not coupled yet.\")`, and the march entry in `participant.py` raises the same.\n\n**Why.** A second-order problem marches the state `[u; v]` (displacement and velocity). The coupling's window state and the frames each participant returns hold `u` only. A window therefore cannot restart from where the last one ended, because `v` is lost between windows.\n\n**Reproduce.** Build a `jno.fem` problem with a `u.tt` term (elastic wave or structural dynamics), give it a time domain, and couple it to another transient problem with `jno.core([...]).solve(coupling=jno.solve.coupling())`. The call reaches `Participant.initial()` and raises.\n\n**Where.** `jno/_coupling/participant.py`: `initial()` and the transient march (both check `problem._time_order == 2`). Also the window loop in `jno/_coupling/driver.py`, which carries one state vector per participant between windows. `docs/coupling.md` lists this under Limitations.\n\n**Direction.** The window state of such a participant needs the full `[u; v]`, restarted from both. The interface frames the other side reads should stay `u` (and the flux or load derived from it). The window state and the frames must therefore be separated, which today they are not. Open questions: whether a dedicated structural scheme (Newmark-type) enters the same window interface as the first-order schemes, and how the window mean of an interface quantity is formed for `u` versus `v`.","files":null}]}